  "worked_solution": "Recall that $[T]_{B|C}$ is the matrix whose columns are the coordinates of the vectors $T({b}_{1}),$ $T({b}_{2}),$ and $T({b}_{3})$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nFirst, we need to find the coordinates of $T({b}_{i})$ relative to the basis $C$ for each $i=1,2,3.$ We can do this simultaneously by considering the following matrix:\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & T({b}_{1}) & T({b}_{2}) & T({b}_{3}) \\\\ | & | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce the left-hand side to reduced row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -6 & 3 & 6 & 18 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -9 & -3 & 27\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 3 & -3 & -9 & -3 & 27\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}-3{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 3 & -12 & -9 & 9\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 & 2 & 6 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \u223c[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -7 & -4 & 12 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the right-hand side gives the matrix of the linear transformation relative to the bases $B$ and $C:$\n\n$$\n[T]_{B|C}=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -4 & 12 \\\\ -4 & -3 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
